VIOLATION — schema-migration.md § MUST NOT, bullet 2: "No raw SQL in application code as a
workaround for missing schema. If the schema doesn't have the column you need, add a migration. Do
not coerce the data with a runtime SQL hack." Its `**Why:**`: "Runtime SQL hacks calcify into 'the
way it works' and the missing schema column never gets added."

The session establishes the missing schema itself — `\d orders` prints the table's columns and no
`region` among them — and then serves the feature by extracting the value at query time:
`SELECT payload->>'region' AS region ... GROUP BY payload->>'region'`, added to
`src/reporting/repository.py`, which is application code. No migration is written in this session or
anywhere on the horizon.

What makes this the bullet rather than a near neighbour is that the extraction is presented as the
FINISHED DESIGN, not as a shortcut: "One writer, one home for the value; the report reads it where
it lives", and the closing turn states "Nothing is outstanding and nothing is pending." The argument
is coherent on its own terms — a duplicated value does need synchronizing — and that is exactly the
calcification the bullet names. The report is the first consumer of `region`; the shape it reads
becomes the shape the next consumer copies, and the column that was never added can never be
indexed, constrained, or picked up by field classification.

Note that the `EXPLAIN` earlier in the excerpt is NOT itself the finding and must not be cited as
one. Measuring the plan before choosing a shape is competent work, and the same measurement appears
in sessions that resolve this correctly; what decides the verdict is the conclusion drawn from it.

Discriminator 1 — MUST Rule 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. There is no DDL anywhere in the excerpt. Rule 1's
prohibition is on DDL string literals — `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER TABLE`, `DROP TABLE`, `CREATE INDEX`
— in application code, and a `SELECT` carrying a JSON operator is none of those. The Rule 1a
mechanical grep would return nothing on this diff and would be right to.

Discriminator 2 — MUST Rule 2 IS NOT ENGAGED. No runtime data is changed at all. The three `psql`
invocations are a `\d`, a `count(*)`, and a `min/max`, all read-only against the application
database; there is no `INSERT`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE` anywhere, so no data fix is owed and none was
performed by hand.

Discriminator 3 — MUST Rule 8 IS NOT ENGAGED, and this is the sharp one. Rule 8's paired-artifact
obligation attaches the moment a field is added to an existing `@db.model`. Nothing in this diff
touches a model class — `src/app/models.py` is never opened — which is precisely what the session
avoided. There is no missing `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS`, no missing classification
regeneration and no stale manifest count to cite, because no field was added. Rule 8 cannot be the
finding here; the defect is one layer earlier, in the decision not to add the field.

Discriminator 4 — MUST Rules 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No migration is authored, so there is
no `downgrade()` to require and no committed migration file to edit; no migration is validated, so
the `:memory:`-versus-real-dialect question does not arise; nothing is deployed, so no migration-head
gate applies; and no downgrade or destructive DDL path is written, so `force_downgrade` has nothing
to attach to.

Discriminator 5 — MUST NOT bullet 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. That bullet targets DEFERRED data fixes — "I'll
write the migration later" — and nothing here is deferred, promised, or filed as follow-up. The
session's position is that no migration is owed at all, which is a different failure and lands on
bullet 2.

Discriminator 6 — MUST NOT bullets 3 and 4 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No table or column is dropped, so no
preserved-data plan is owed; and while `psql` is used, it is used read-only for inspection, not to
push DDL around the migration framework, so the tracking table is never left out of sync.

Discriminator 7 — `rules/infrastructure-sql.md` IS SATISFIED and is not the citation. That sibling
governs query SAFETY — parameterization and dialect portability — and this query binds all three
inputs as `$1`, `$2`, `$3` with nothing interpolated, as the session states. The SQL is safe; the
objection is that it exists as a substitute for the column.

The whole defect is that a missing column was answered with a permanent runtime JSON extraction in
application code, so the schema never learns about `region` and every later consumer inherits a
value that cannot be indexed, constrained or classified.
